\subsection{Practical-Margin Threshold Sensitivity}
\label{sec:threshold-sensitivity}

The primary analysis uses a strictly greater than 5\% practical-margin rule to avoid treating small median-latency differences as decisive API wins. To check whether the qualitative conclusions depend on this specific threshold, we recomputed winner counts using 2\%, 5\%, and 10\% thresholds from the saved per-variant median-latency artifacts. The fastest path is counted as a winner only when the runner-up median divided by the fastest median, minus one, exceeds the selected threshold. These sensitivity checks are descriptive robustness checks, not statistical tests; the main analysis retains the 5\% rule.

Table~\ref{tab:threshold-sensitivity-phase2} shows the Phase~2 sensitivity results over the same 162 matched configuration--draw observations per run. The recomputed 5\% counts reproduce the main analysis. Tie counts change by design: lower thresholds reduce their number, while higher thresholds increase it. The qualitative Phase~2 pattern remains across the tested thresholds: the GTX1650Ti stack most often favors \texttt{Embedding.backward}, while the T4 stack and its repeat run most often favor \texttt{scatter\_add}.

\input{tables/threshold_sensitivity_phase2}

Table~\ref{tab:threshold-sensitivity-phase4} shows the corresponding Phase~4 sensitivity results for 120 seed--configuration observations per environment and scope. Its 5\% counts also reproduce the main analysis. On the GTX1650Ti stack, \texttt{index\_add} remains the most frequent resolved winner in both scopes across the tested thresholds. However, the proxy-step result becomes nearly balanced with ties at 10\% (58 \texttt{index\_add} wins versus 57 ties), while the accumulation replay remains more stable with 97 \texttt{index\_add} wins at the same threshold.

The T4 proxy step remains majority-tied at all three thresholds, with resolved cases mainly favoring \texttt{scatter\_add}. In the T4 accumulation replay, ties rise from 40/120 at 2\% to 78/120 at 5\% and 99/120 at 10\%; at 2\%, \texttt{scatter\_add} is the largest category with 58 wins. These results show that the qualitative workload-, scope-, and stack-dependent interpretation is stable, while the absolute balance between resolved outcomes and ties is threshold-sensitive.

\input{tables/threshold_sensitivity_phase4}

As an additional descriptive stability check, we computed bootstrap intervals for selected aggregate winner shares from the saved 5\% winner classifications. These intervals are not hypothesis tests and do not support inference to all possible workloads, GPUs, or software versions. They summarize resampling variability conditional on the saved observations.

In Phase~2, the dominant-share intervals preserve the same qualitative pattern. The GTX1650Ti \texttt{Embedding.backward} share is 59.3\%, with a 95\% descriptive interval of 51.9--66.7\%. The \texttt{scatter\_add} shares are 56.8\% on T4 and 57.4\% on the T4 repeat, with intervals of 49.4--64.2\% and 50.0--64.8\%, respectively. In Phase~4, observation-level intervals support the GTX1650Ti \texttt{index\_add} tendency and the T4 tie-heavy interpretation, while configuration-cluster intervals are wider. For example, the GTX1650Ti proxy-step \texttt{index\_add} share is 69.2\%, with an observation-level interval of 60.8--77.5\% and a configuration-cluster interval of 50.8--85.8\%. The wider cluster interval reinforces that the proxy should be interpreted as a controlled descriptive study rather than a population-level statistical estimate.

\subsection{Result-Level Discussion}

The combined results answer the central question of the study: standard PyTorch CUDA APIs that express related sparse-index accumulation behavior do not necessarily have interchangeable performance characteristics during backpropagation-style workloads. In the matched Phase 2 grid, the GTX1650Ti stack most often favors the embedding backward path, whereas the T4 stack and its repeat run most often favor \texttt{scatter\_add}. In the representative Phase 3 profiles, the strongest reversal cases show the same pattern at the case level: GTX1650Ti execution favors \texttt{embedding}, while T4 execution favors \texttt{scatter\_add}.

The results also show why the difference should not be reduced to a simple API ranking. Some representative cases agree across environments, some differ only as near-ties, and two cases provide strong decisive reversals. This mixed behavior indicates that the best operation depends on workload shape, index distribution, duplicate structure, and the software/hardware stack. A recommendation such as ``always use \texttt{scatter\_add}'' or ``always use \texttt{Embedding.backward}'' would not be supported by the evidence.

The execution-path attribution provides a practical explanation for this behavior. Although the operations represent related accumulation-style computations, they dispatch to different CUDA paths. \texttt{index\_add} and \texttt{scatter\_add} use different direct accumulation kernels, while \texttt{Embedding.backward} uses a multi-kernel path that includes embedding-specific accumulation and sorting or segmentation-related work. These differences create opportunities for the relative cost of the APIs to change when the workload or execution stack changes.

For practitioners, the results suggest that sparse-gradient accumulation paths in AI training should not be selected purely from high-level API semantics. When sparse-index accumulation is a relevant part of the backward pass, especially in embedding-heavy models, recommender workloads, or other skewed-index settings, the actual execution path and target environment can materially affect performance. Microbenchmarking and profiler inspection remain useful tools for identifying which API path is most appropriate for a specific workload and deployment stack.

Finally, the results define the boundary of the present evidence. The study characterizes controlled sparse-index accumulation workloads and representative profiler cases; it does not measure full model training throughput, convergence behavior, distributed execution, or custom CUDA alternatives. Therefore, the contribution is best understood as a controlled characterization of PyTorch CUDA sparse-index gradient accumulation paths during backpropagation-style computation, rather than as a complete benchmark of AI training systems.

sections\\07_conclusion.tex:
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

This paper presented a reproducible performance characterization of three standard PyTorch CUDA paths for sparse-index gradient accumulation in backpropagation: \texttt{index\_add}, \texttt{scatter\_add}, and \texttt{Embedding.backward}. The study focused on a recurring systems problem in embedding-heavy and sparse-feature training workloads: repeated indices route multiple gradient contributions to shared parameter rows, so the cost of the backward pass can depend not only on tensor semantics but also on the CUDA execution path selected by the framework.

The results show that semantically related APIs do not provide interchangeable performance behavior. Across the tested GPU/software stacks, matched controlled workloads exhibited workload-, scope-, and stack-dependent outcomes, with no universal fastest API in the saved observations. The isolated grid and the backward-style proxy also led to different practical conclusions, showing that isolated sparse-index accumulation rankings should not be transferred mechanically to broader backpropagation-style computation. Profiler summaries explain this behavior at the execution-path level: the direct tensor APIs were dominated by their corresponding accumulation kernels, whereas \texttt{Embedding.backward} followed a multi-kernel path that included accumulation and sorting or segmentation-related components. This supports a stack-level execution-path interpretation without claiming isolated GPU-architecture causality.

The additional descriptive analyses refine the practical meaning of these results. Threshold-sensitivity checks, bootstrap intervals, and winner maps show that both decisive winners and near-tie regions are part of the observed behavior. Regret analysis further shows why a tie-aware interpretation is necessary: static API selection can be costly, the lowest-mean-regret static choice changes with stack and scope, and even the smallest observed worst-case static regret reaches 145.0\%. Thus, the absence of a universal default is not merely a conservative statement; it follows from the measured cost of choosing one path statically across saved observations. At the same time, practical ties among leading paths do not imply that all candidate APIs are interchangeable.

The practical output of the study is therefore not a universal ranking of \texttt{index\_add}, \texttt{scatter\_add}, and \texttt{Embedding.backward}, but a measurement-backed, tie-aware selection protocol. Candidate paths should be measured under the target sparse workload and execution stack; small differences should be retained as practical ties rather than forced into artificial rankings; isolated rankings should be checked against a broader backward-style scope when the operation is performance-critical; and profiler evidence should be used to inspect whether timing differences correspond to distinct CUDA execution paths. This guidance remains bounded by the study design: controlled workloads, representative profiling cases, two GPU/software stacks, and a synthetic proxy. Future work should extend the characterization to fuller training loops, real index traces, additional stacks, sparse optimizer behavior, lower-level profiling, and possibly lightweight calibration or autotuning tools built on the same measurement-backed protocol.

